{"id": "2f2a298a-8238-482a-98e0-b95a43114282", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T15:23:21.583720", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "bc7e21c5-0c9b-4667-9a4d-8f9e3f379a58", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Confirmed: no zero-bubble pipeline parallelism repo exists in the masked environment. Those grep hits are hex-string coincidences (e.g., \"1f1b\" in unicode tables / lock files).\n\nSo the deliverable: report that repository is absent (masked), everything about file/symbol entry points is \"unknown\". Then propose a design-only plan based on the well-known GPipe/1F1B/ZB-H (Zero-Bubble Pipeline Parallelism, Qi et al. 2024 \"Zero Bubble Pipeline Parallelism\") analytical model, with closed-form makespan formulas, plus a discrete-event simulator we would write ourselves (as a harness) — since no repo code exists, the design must be simulator-based and explicitly label the model as reconstructed, not repo-verified.\n\nLet me be careful and thorough about correctness of the analytical schedules, since the design hinges on them.\n\nKnown results (uniform model: p stages, m microbatches, forward cost per microbatch f, backward cost b, no interleaved vs interleaved; ZB-HV (ZB-H) and ZB-1F1B (ZB-1p?) hmm.\n\nNaming: In the Zero Bubble Pipeline Parallelism paper (Qi et al., arXiv 2401.10241), schedulers: ZB-HV (zero-bubble, using backward-forward-backward \"bubble\" scheduling, needs virtual backward microbatch), ZB-1F1B (zero-bubble variant of 1F1B, ZB-1p might mean \"zero bubble 1 placement\"?). Hmm. In some repos (e.g., \"pipe\" / \"zero_bubble\" implementations), the methods listed: 1F1B, 1F1B-I (interleaved 1F1B, i.e., virtual stages), ZB-HV, ZB-1F1B. And \"ZB-2p\"? Possibly \"zero bubble 2 pass\"? Hmm.\n\nActually there's a repository \"yuqi1129/ZeroBubblePipelineParallelism\" (GitHub) which implements GPipe, 1F1B, interleaved 1F1B (1F1B-I), ZB-HV, ZB-1F1B with a simulator. Also \"pipe-deep\" ... Another repo: \"zero_bubble\" in some project. The question's names: 1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p, ZB-2p. Hmm, \"ZB-1p\" and \"ZB-2p\" could be \"ZB-1F1B\" and \"ZB-HV\"? Or \"ZB-V\" ... Hmm. There is a notation in some codebases: ZB-1P = zero-bubble with 1 placement, ZB-2P = with 2 placements? Or perhaps in \"Megatron-like\" scheduler naming: \"ZB-1F1B (ZB-1p)\" and \"ZB-HV (ZB-2p)\"? Hmm, actually there's the \"Zero Bubble Pipeline Parallelism\" paper which has schedulers ZB-HV and ZB-1F1B; and another related naming, e.g., in \"Bonsai\" or \"ZipStream\"? Not important — I'll note that the mapping of ZB-1p/ZB-2p to ZB-1F1B/ZB-HV (or to \"1-pass/2-pass memory placements\") is unknown from repo and must be confirmed with the researcher / treated as assumption.\n\nBetter: ask the researcher — but question_budget is 6 and there are no topics given. I can include clarification questions in the plan's uncertainties. The researcher topics list is empty; question_budget 6 means I can ask up to 6 questions in total (via final answer text presumably). I'll list targeted questions.\n\nNow the analytical model. Let me write the standard formulas (uniform, no communication overhead):\n\nLet p = number of stages, m = microbatches, per-stage forward time F, backward time B (B = 2F typically). Total iteration = sum over m microbatches of (F+B) work per stage = m(F+B) minimum (\"perfect\" time).\n\n1F1B makespan (uniform): T_1F1B = (F+B)(m + p - 1). Bubble ratio = (p-1)/(m+p-1). So throughput improves with m; monotonic increasing in m.\n\nInterleaved 1F1B (1F1B-I, v virtual stages/chunks per stage, v = p/m? Actually in GPipe-style interleaving with v chunks per device): T_1F1BI = (F+B)(m + (p-1)/v) roughly, since effective pipeline depth becomes p*v with per-chunk time (F+B)/v → bubble = (p-1)/v * (F+B) → T = (F+B)(m + (p-1)/v) when m divisible by v. Monotonic in m too, better constant term.\n\nZB-1F1B: zero bubble → T = m(F+B) exactly, when m >= p-1 (condition: number of microbatches must be at least p-1? The paper requires m ≥ p-1 for ZB-1F1B to achieve zero bubble? Actually ZB-HV requires no constraint, ZB-1F1B requires m ≥ p-1? Let me recall: ZB-HV works with any m; ZB-1F1B has requirement... In the paper, ZB-1F1B has \"the condition that the number of microbatches m ≥ p-1\"? Hmm, I recall ZB-HV needs virtual backward and works for all m; ZB-1F1B achieves zero bubble with memory similar to 1F1B and requires m ≥ p-1? I'm not fully certain — flag as assumption to verify in code.\n\nZB-HV: T = m(F+B), zero bubble for all m.\n\nIf ZB-1p = ZB-1F1B and ZB-2p = ZB-HV? Not clear. Alternatively ZB-1p/ZB-2p could mean \"zero-bubble with 1 or 2 microbatch placements/passing\" — hmm.\n\nHmm — maybe the repo in question is \"sikezic/ZeroBubblepp\" or \"zero-bubble-pp\"? Or maybe it's a repo where the scheduler names literally include ZB-1p and ZB-2p... Actually! There is \"ZB-1P\" and \"ZB-2P\" hmm... There's the \"Zero Bubble Pipeline Parallelism\" naming: ZB-HV, ZB-1F1B. But some implementations (e.g., in TorchTitan? \"zero_bubble\" schedule) hmm. Another: \"Bolt\" / \"Bifrost\". Or the naming in the paper \"Efficient low-overhead pipeline parallelism\"? Hmm.\n\nWait — I recall in some code (e.g., \"zerobubble\" implementations in \"pipe-it\" or \"DynaPipe\") ... \"1p\" and \"2p\" might refer to number of \"passes\" over microbatches: ZB-1p = one-pass (each device holds one microbatch's activation at a time), ZB-2p = two-pass. I'll treat this as unknown/assumption and design so both interpretations map: I'll parameterize ZB-1p and ZB-2p abstractly with per-method makespan models and a \"zero-bubble condition\" parameter, and ask the researcher for the exact source-of-truth definition (or, since repo is absent, for permission to use the paper's ZB-1F1B/ZB-HV formulas as the reference model).\n\nGiven the task is design-only and repos aren't required (\"track.md: Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\"), the design should be a closed-form/simulator experiment with clear formulas, monotonicity checks, and relative-efficiency thresholds.\n\nDesign specifics:\n\nFactors:\n- Method (4 levels): 1F1B, 1F1B-I (v=2 or v=p), ZB-1p, ZB-2p\n- Microbatch count m: {4, 8, 16, 32, 64} (maybe include 2 to test \"fewer microbatches\"; use {2,4,8,16,32,64}), with m a multiple of v for 1F1B-I fairness, or note divisibility handling.\n- Stage count p: {4, 8, 16}? Primary fixed p=8; secondary sweep as robustness check.\n- per-microbatch forward time F: derived from model size & hardware throughput OR fixed unit; per-stage model size fixed via parameter-preserving split (model size fixed, so per-microbatch F fixed as long as per-stage layer count and hidden dims fixed). Two settings: uniform layers; and GPT-style dims (d_model, n_layers, etc.) for tokens/sec conversion.\n\nThroughput metric: samples/sec = m * B_mb (microbatch samples in tokens?) Actually convention: throughput = (m * micro_batch_tokens * p?) hmm. Define: tokens/sec = (total tokens processed per iteration) / (iteration time) = (m * t_mb) / T_iter. Also relative efficiency = T_min / T_method where T_min = m(F+B) (perfect pipeline). And bubble ratio = 1 - efficiency.\n\nDecision rules:\n- Monotonic improvement: throughput(m) strictly non-decreasing across the m grid and, more strictly, throughput(m_{k+1}) ≥ 1.02 × throughput(m_k) for the coarse-to-fine range; check Spearman ρ = 1.0 per method with ρ threshold ≥0.95; plus report marginal gains diminishing (concavity check).\n- ZB-2p \"high throughput with fewer microbatches\": define efficiency_ZB2p(m_min) ≥ 0.9 (i.e., within 10% of perfect) at m = p-1 or smaller (m ≤ p/2), and efficiency ratio vs 1F1B at same m: eff_ZB2p(m)/eff_1F1B(m) ≥ 1.2, and absolute gap ≥ 0.15.\n\nControls (same for all methods): p, m grid, per-stage compute (layers/dims), F/B ratio (B=2F), communication/activation-transfer time, no recomputation vs recompute-off (or fixed), warmup handling: use steady-state makespan of a single iteration after warmup (or analytic steady state = infinite-stream model), memory constraint tracked but not binding (report peak activation memory, flag OOS), same microbatch token count.\n\nAnalysis plan: table of makespan & throughput per (method, m, p), plots throughput vs m per method, efficiency vs m, monotonicity test, threshold table, and a sensitivity sweep over F:B ratio and comm time.\n\nFeasibility: no GPU, no numpy/torch, no repo → implement a pure-Python discrete-event simulator + closed-form checks; validation via (a) closed-form vs simulated makespan agreement (unit tests / self-consistency invariants: no device double-booking, per-microbatch F before B, all microbatches complete), (b) reproduce known bubble ratios (p-1)/(m+p-1).\n\nAlso: the \"answer\" to the question is expected as yes/no mapping.\n\nNow let me also decide whether to actually verify formulas — I could write a small closed-form calc script to sanity check monotonicity numbers (pure python, no numpy). That is feasible and would strengthen the design (feasible checks). But instruction says design-only; no experiments required. A quick numerical sanity check of the formulas is a \"feasible check\", which is fine and cheap. Maybe I'll do it as part of the plan? Plan stage: I should keep it fairly short in plan stage, then result stage with full design. Let me do a tiny numeric sanity check now to include in evidence for the plan (it validates the monotonicity decision rule thresholds, e.g., efficiency values at m=4..64 for p=8).\n\nLet me compute quickly.\n\nefficiency 1F1B: m/(m+p-1). p=8: m=4→4/11=0.364; 8→8/15=0.533; 16→16/23=0.696; 32→32/39=0.821; 64→64/71=0.901.\n1F1B-I with v=2: m/(m+(p-1)/v)= m/(m+3.5): 4→0.533; 8→0.696;16→0.821;32→0.901;64→0.948.\nZB (zero bubble, m≥p-1): 1.0 (if m≥7). At m=4 (<p-1) zero bubble may not be attainable → note.\n\nSo at m=4, ZB efficiency 1.0 vs 1F1B 0.364 → ratio 2.75. Good.\n\nLet me run a quick script to produce these numbers to include in evidence, so the design's thresholds are grounded.\n\nAlso check: does m need to be multiple of v? For 1F1B-I with v chunks per stage, GPipe-interleaved bubble = (p-1)/v*(F+B) requires m*v divisible by p? Standard: requires m % v == 0. I'll require m % v == 0 and use v in {1,2,4} with m multiples of 4 (m ∈ {4,8,16,32,64}) so all v divide m. Good — that's a control/fairness detail.\n\nLet me run the sanity computation.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "cat > /tmp/sanity.py <<'EOF'\np_list=[4,8]\nms=[4,8,16,32,64]\ndef eff_1f1b(p,m): return m/(m+p-1)\ndef eff_1f1bi(p,m,v): return m/(m+(p-1)/v) if m%v==0 else float('nan')\ndef eff_zb(p,m): return 1.0 if m>=p-1 else None\nfor p in p_list:\n  print(\"p=\",p)\n  for m in ms:\n    print(\"  m=%2d 1F1B=%.3f 1F1B-I(v=2)=%.3f v4=%.3f ZB(zero-bubble attainable if m>=p-1=%s)\"%(\n      m,eff_1f1b(p,m),eff_1f1bi(p,m,2),eff_1f1bi(p,m,4),m>=p-1))\n# marginal gain ratios for 1F1B at p=8\np=8\nprev=None\nfor m in ms:\n    e=eff_1f1b(p,m)\n    if prev: print(\"p=8 m=%d eff=%.4f gain_vs_prev=%.1f%%\"%(m,e,100*(e/prev-1)))\n    prev=e\nEOF\npython3 /tmp/sanity.py", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b8b2a3aa38945b02", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-b8b2a3aa38945b02", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cat > /tmp/sanity.py <<'EOF'\\np_list=[4,8]\\nms=[4,8,16,32,64]\\ndef eff_1f1b(p,m): return m/(m+p-1)\\ndef eff_1f1bi(p,m,v): return m/(m+(p-1)/v) if m%v==0 else float('nan')\\ndef eff_zb(p,m): return 1.0 if m>=p-1 else None\\nfor p in p_list:\\n  print(\\\"p=\\\",p)\\n  for m in ms:\\n    print(\\\"  m=%2d 1F1B=%.3f 1F1B-I(v=2)=%.3f v4=%.3f ZB(zero-bubble attainable if m>=p-1=%s)\\\"%(\\n      m,eff_1f1b(p,m),eff_1f1bi(p,m,2),eff_1f1bi(p,m,4),m>=p-1))\\n# marginal gain ratios for 1F1B at p=8\\np=8\\nprev=None\\nfor m in ms:\\n    e=eff_1f1b(p,m)\\n    if prev: print(\\\"p=8 m=%d eff=%.4f gain_vs_prev=%.1f%%\\\"%(m,e,100*(e/prev-1)))\\n    prev=e\\