d is comfortably above noise and well below expected effect.\\n\\n## 2) Proposed approach (design only \\u2014 nothing executed, nothing claimed as measured)\\n\\n**Design type:** deterministic analytical + discrete-event simulation study of four schedule graphs; no training run, no GPU. (This harness would be new code, because the workspace has no reusable script.)\\n\\n- **Factors.** Within-subject factor: microbatch count `m \\u2208 {2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64}` (all multiples of chunk factor `v`, so 1F1B-I divisibility never becomes a confound). Between factor: method \\u2208 {1F1B, 1F1B-I (v = 2 fixed), ZB-1p, ZB-2p}.\\n- **Fixed setting (held constant across all methods):** pipeline depth p = 8 stages = 8 devices; one model configuration so per-stage forward cost `F` is identical for every method; backward cost `B = 2F`; activation-communication cost per stage-to-stage hop fixed (`c`), recomputation policy fixed (default: no recompute; sensitivity run with recompute); `m\\u00b7microbatch tokens` = same global batch \\u2192 **same total work per iteration for every method** (this is what makes throughput comparable).\\n- **Metric.** Primary: iteration makespan `T(method, m, p)` from the schedule. Derived, all reported together: (a) relative efficiency `E = m(F+B)/T` (dimensionless, convention-free), (b) bubble ratio `1 \\u2212 E`, (c) throughput `tokens/s = m\\u00b7tokens_mb / T` and `samples/s`, with `F` instantiated from a stated device throughput. **Steady state**: makespan of a post-warmup iteration (analytic infinite-stream / schedule repeated, first ramp excluded).\\n- **Analysis & decision rules (pre-registered):**\\n  - *Monotonic improvement* for 1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p: `T` strictly decreasing-in-E as m grows; Spearman \\u03c1(throughput, m) = 1.0 **and** every consecutive step gain \\u2265 +2 % (expected 10\\u201347 % at p=8, so a true claim clears it easily). Report concavity/diminishing returns as secondary.\\n  - *ZB-2p \\\"high throughput with fewer microbatches\\\"*: at low m (`m \\u2264 p/2`, i.e. m = 2, 4): `E_ZB-2p \\u2265 0.90` **and** `E_ZB-2p / E_1F1B \\u2265 1.2` **and** absolute gap `\\u2265 0.15`. (Probe shows E_1F1B(m=4,p=8) = 0.364, so ZB-2p only needs to be near-zero-bubble to pass.)\\n  - *Ordering / no-crossing check*: E_ZB \\u2265 E_1F1B-I > E_1F1B at every m (sanity of implementation).\\n  - *Sensitivity*: p \\u2208 {4, 8}, v \\u2208 {1, 2, 4}, `c/F \\u2208 {0, 0.1, 0.3}` \\u2014 the conclusion must survive all cells to be reported as robust.\\n- **Feasibility / validity basis without GPU:** deterministic, no RNG, runs in seconds on the 224-core CPU host using only stdlib; validity comes from (i) closed-form makespan formulas, (ii) an independently written event-based simulator that must agree with the formula, and (iii) invariants (no device overlap, F-before-B per microbatch, all m microbatches complete, per-stage work conserved). Known-answer validation: reproduce the textbook bubble ratio `(p\\u22121)/(m+p\\u22121)` for 1F1B before trusting the ZB schedules. Evidence level is stated as *analytical model*, never as measured hardware throughput.\\n\\n## 3) Consequential assumptions (NOT confirmed by anything in the workspace)\\n\\n1. **A1 \\u2014 schedule semantics of ZB-1p / ZB-2p.** Repo absent; I assume the \\\"zero-bubble\\\" family semantics (1p \\u2248 ZB-1F1B-like placement, 2p \\u2248 HV/virtual-backward placement giving bubble-free execution for *any* m), i.e. the mechanism that would make ZB-2p strong at small m. If instead \\\"1p/2p\\\" means something else (1-pass/2-pass, 1 or 2 in-flight microbatches per device, or \\\"1/2 p\\\" = memory placement), the ZB-2p decision rule must be re-anchored. **Needs your confirmation.**\\n2. **A2 \\u2014 microbatch grid.** `{2,4,8,16,32,64}` with `v | m` is my choice, not a repo default. m must also stay \\u22651 and typically \\u2265 p\\u22121 for ZB-1p zero-bubble to be attainable, so m = 2/4 may be a *constraint-violating-but-legal* cell \\u2014 reported, not deleted.\\n3. **A3 \\u2014 \\\"high throughput\\\" baseline.** I propose the *primary* baseline be **relative-to-perfect efficiency E** (absolute, model-based), with the 1F1B-relative ratio as a *secondary* comparator. If you want the claim defined purely as \\\"\\u2265 X % of 1F1B at same m\\\", thresholds change.\\n4. **A4 \\u2014 memory.** Treated as **reported, not binding** (peak activation memory tracked per method; ZB schedules typically cost more memory, so any cell exceeding a stated per-device budget is *flagged* and re-tested at smaller microbatch size rather than silently dropped). Tell me if memory should instead be a hard eligibility filter.\\n5. **A5 \\u2014 steady state vs per-step.** I use post-warmup steady-state makespan (analytic repeated schedule); no first-iteration ramp, no optimizer-step time, no I/O, no checkpointing. If you want measured per-step throughput including the first iteration, a warmup/averaging rule (e.g. drop first iteration, report median of 20) must be pre-declared.\\n6. **A6 \\u2014 model/units.** One fixed GPT-style model config (n_layers divisible by p; layer split equal across stages) and `B = 2F` are assumptions; the tokens/s \\u2192 absolute-scale mapping (device TFLOPs, MFU) is a free parameter and is *not* used for the pass/fail decision.\\n7. **A7 \\u2014 repo provenance.** All \\\"implementation\\\" statements about the repository are unverifiable in this workspace; the deliverable is explicitly a model-level design that becomes executable-as-is once the real repo/GPU is available (same m-grid, same controls, same thresholds), at which point schedule-derived `T` is replaced by measured iteration time.\\n\\n**Questions before I write the full design (6 max, here are 6):** (Q1) Confirm ZB-1p vs ZB-2p semantics/definitions intended here (and repo name/commit if source access can be restored). (Q2) Approve the m-grid `{2,4,8,16,32,64}` and `v = 2` for 1F1B-I, or give repo defaults. (Q3) Is \\\"high throughput\\\" anchored on absolute efficiency (my proposal, \\u2265 0.90) or on ratio-to-1F1B? (Q4) Is peak memory a reported diagnostic or a hard eligibility constraint? (Q5) Steady-state makespan or measured per-step throughput with warmup policy? (Q6) Required final unit/convention (tokens/s, samples/s, TFLOPs, or dimensionless efficiency).\\n\\nAwaiting approval before producing the complete design (stage=result).\", \"evidence\": \"Verified inspection (commands actually run, no GPU/deps needed):\\n1. `ls -la /workspace` \\u2192 only `-rw------- task.json` (651 B). `inspect_repository(\\\".\\\")` \\u2192 `task.json`. No source files at all.\\n2. `cat /workspace/task.json` \\u2192 only the question/context + note \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"\\n3. `ls -la /`; `/home/actor` empty; `/opt`, `/srv`, `/data`, `/repo` absent.\\n4. `find / -xdev -iname \\\"*zero*bubble*\\\" -o -iname \\\"*pipe*parallel*\\\"` \\u2192 0 results. `grep -ril \\\"1f1b\\\" /usr/lib/python3*/ /usr/local/lib` \\u2192 only hex-string false positives (idna uts46data.py, ansible FILES.json/poetry.lock). => no 1F1B/ZB implementation reachable.\\n5. `python3 --version` \\u2192 3.14.4; `pip3 list` \\u2192 Ubuntu system packages only (no torch, numpy, tensorflow, jax, ray); `python3 -c \\\"import torch\\\"` and `import numpy` \\u2192 ModuleNotFoundError. Environment: gpu_enabled=false, network disabled \\u21d2 cannot install frameworks.\\n6. Closed-form sanity probe (`/tmp/sanity.py`, pure stdlib, exit 0): relative efficiency at p=8 \\u2014 1F1B: 0.364(m=4), 0.533(8), 0.696(16), 0.821(32), 0.901(64) with step gains +46.7 %, +30.4 %, +17.9 %, +9.9 %; 1F1B-I v=2: 0.533\\u21920.948; v=4: 0.696\\u21920.973; at p=4, 1F1B: 0.571\\u21920.955. Confirms (a) monotonic improvement is large and easily threshold-testable, (b) small-m cells (m=4,p=8) give a discriminating regime for ZB-2p (E_1F1B = 0.364).\\n7. Feasibility logic: throughput vs microbatch count for these schedules is determined by schedule structure (bubble length \\u221d p\\u22121 vs m), hence answerable analytically/event-simulated with stdlib arithmetic; no measured outcome is invented anywhere in this turn.\", \"assumptions\": [\"ZB-1p and ZB-2p semantics are unverified (repo absent); assumed zero-bubble family variants where the 2p variant reaches near-zero bubble for any microbatch count, which is the mechanism the question's small-m claim depends on\", \"Microbatch grid {2,4,8,16,32,64} with v|m, pipeline depth p=8 devices, and interleaving factor v=2 for 1F1B-I are designer choices, not repo defaults\", \"Throughput metric (makespan -> tokens/s plus dimensionless relative efficiency E) is defined by this design because the repo defines none\", \"Backward cost B=2F, equal layer split across stages, no recomputation (sensitivity only), fixed activation-transfer cost c: modelling assumptions, not repo facts\", \"Memory is a reported diagnostic rather than a hard constraint, so no cell is dropped for memory reasons\", \"Steady-state (post-warmup, analytic repeated-schedule) makespan is used; first-iteration ramp, optimizer step, I/O and checkpointing are excluded\", \"No GPU measurement exists; the deliverable is model-level design evidence, convertible to measured iteration time if repo/GPU become available\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Exact file+symbol entry points, microbatch-count argument name, and whether the repo ships its own simulator/cost model are all UNKNOWN \\u2014 no code is present in /workspace\", \"Whether ZB-1p requires m >= p-1 to be bubble-free while ZB-2p does not (the likely source of the claimed small-m advantage) \\u2014 must be confirmed from the paper/source you approve\", \"Definition of 'high throughput' (absolute efficiency >= 0.90 vs ratio-to-1F1B >= 1.2) and of 'improvement' (>= 2% step gain vs Spearman rho = 1) are pending your approval\", \"Whether memory pressure caps the ZB-2p small-m advantage in a real run (model may under-report memory cost)\", \"Repo's native throughput convention (tokens/s vs samples/s vs TFLOPs vs iteration time) and default pipeline depth/device count\", \"Whether communication time is included in the repo's schedules; my c/F sensitivity covers but does not resolve this\", \"Real-hardware deviations (kernel variance, stragglers, memory-constrained recompute) are outside model scope, so model-based pass/fail may not transfer 1:1 to a GPU run\"]}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
