{"id": "c9681e61-7d45-4071-8d24-bd0ca6cbbc82", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T15:26:56.382376", "source": "user", "parent_id": "a09bd57e-d37b-41ca-a40e-44bfe645e2ab", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach approved — inspection findings accepted (repo absent, stdlib-only analytical/event-simulation design is the right call for a design-only task; good discipline not inventing file paths). Ye rahe tumhare 6 sawaalon ke answers, phir full design maango:\\n\\nQ1 (ZB semantics): Repo se confirm karna possible nahi hai, isliye isse UNKNOWN hi rehne do. Canonical zero-bubble-schedule-family semantics (ZB-1p: split-backward/1-place variant jismein zero bubble ke liye structural condition hoti hai; ZB-2p: virtual/stage-split 2-place variant jo kam microbatch par bhi near-zero bubble target karta hai) ko PRIMARY interpretation banao, clearly labeled as assumption A1 with an explicit note that if repo semantics differ, the ZB-2p decision rule must be re-anchored. Don't pick a single hard-coded bubble formula for ZB — derive schedules from a stated construction rule (or cite the structural property) so the design stays honest about what is modelled vs asserted.\\n\\nQ2 (grid): m ∈ {2,4,8,16,32,64} approved; v=2 primary with v ∈ {1,2,4} sensitivity approved. Keep m=2/4 cells for ZB-1p even if constraint-violating, flagged as such — that's exactly the regime the question probes.\\n\\nQ3 (\\\"high throughput\\\" anchor): Your proposal accepted — primary anchor = absolute relative efficiency E ≥ 0.90 at low m (m ≤ p/2), with ratio-to-1F1B (≥1.2× and ≥0.15 absolute gap) as the secondary corroborating comparator. Pre-register both.\\n\\nQ4 (memory): Reported diagnostic, NOT a hard eligibility filter. Track peak activation memory per (method, m) as a feasibility caveat column so a reviewer sees the cost of ZB's throughput; never silently drop a cell.\\n\\nQ5 (steady state): Post-warmup steady-state makespan approved. State the warmup-exclusion rule explicitly in the analysis plan so it transfers unchanged to a future GPU run.\\n\\nQ6 (units): Primary decision metric = dimensionless E (convention-free, since repo defines none); report tokens/s and samples/s alongside via a stated F instantiation, with pass/fail depending only on E.\\n\\nOne addition to the deliverable: include a \\\"claim-to-cell mapping\\\" table — each half of the question (monotone improvement for 1F1B/1F1B-I/ZB-1p; ZB-2p strong at small m) mapped to the exact metric, cells, thresholds, and what an observed failure would mean. Also keep the known-answer validation (textbook 1F1B bubble ratio) as a gating check before trusting ZB schedules.\\n\\nAb full final design do: rationale, fixed controls, factors/levels, metric definitions, pre-registered decision rules with thresholds, sensitivity grid, validation/invariants, memory-diagnostic reporting, feasibility note (stdlib, no GPU, seconds-scale), and the assumptions/uncertainties register. Design hi final deliverable hai — koi result ya measured number mat claim karna.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "759c6afb-03e0-45c3-864b-9687a4487e43", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T15:28:49.778919", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "c9681e61-7d45-4071-8d24-bd0ca6cbbc82", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Now I need to produce the full final design. Let me build it carefully, with:\n\n- Schedule construction rules (derivation, not hard-coded bubble formula) — I should specify the discrete-event simulation rules for each of the 4 schedules precisely enough that the design is executable, and derive makespan from the simulator, with closed-form as cross-check.\n- Known-answer validation gating check.\n- Claim-to-cell mapping table.\n- Memory diagnostic model (GPipe all-forward = m activations per stage; 1F1B = p; 1F1B-I = p... with v chunks; ZB-1F1B ~ similar to 1F1B; ZB-HV ~ 2p-ish... ) I should present memory as a modelled diagnostic with explicit accounting rule (count of in-flight microbatch activations resident per device at any time), derived from the simulated schedule rather than assumed formulas — that's honest: count from the event trace.\n- Metric definitions: T_iter, E = m(F+B)/T, tokens/s = m*tokens_mb/T, samples/s.\n- Decision rules with thresholds and failure interpretation.\n- Sensitivity grid.\n- Feasibility: pure stdlib, event simulation O(events) ~ small.\n\nLet me write the construction rules for the schedules in terms of a DAG + list scheduling with priorities — that is a defensible \"construction rule\" instead of hard-coded bubble formulas:\n\nCommon: p stages numbered 0..p-1 (stage 0 embed, stage p-1 head). m microbatches. Each microbatch has tasks F_i (stage s) and B_i (stage s). Precedence: F_i(s) → F_i(s+1); F_i(p-1) → B_i(p-1); B_i(s) → B_i(s-1). Resource: each stage executes one task at a time (single stream per device). Optional split backward into dB/dW for ZB (ZB-H splits B into dB (input-grad, blocks pipeline) and dW (weight-grad, can fill bubbles)). For ZB-1p (ZB-1F1B-like): backward is split so dW tasks fill the residual bubble slots; structural condition m ≥ p−1 for zero bubble (cite structural property from the ZB paper). For ZB-2p: uses virtual stages/split placements (HV: hybrid with virtual backward microbatch), targeting zero bubble for any m, with higher in-flight activation count.\n\nRather than asserting the paper's exact schedule, I define each method as a priority list-scheduling policy over the same task DAG:\n- 1F1B: greedy ASAP with priority = prefer earlier-stage work; classic warmup (stage s does m−s? ) Actually standard 1F1B: warmup size w_s = min(m, p−1−s); then steady 1F1B; cooldown.\n- 1F1B-I: same but with v virtual chunks per device; stage index becomes virtual stage; per-chunk cost F/v.\n- ZB-1p: task DAG with B split into dB + dW (dW cost = B/2 approx; and dW does not block successors), scheduled with a priority that places dW into idle slots; zero bubble requires m ≥ p−1 (structural).\n- ZB-2p: DAG additionally allowing the \"virtual microbatch\" split (HV) so bubble slots are filled with dW of adjacent microbatches at any m.\n\nThen makespan from event sim; the \"zero bubble\" outcome is a *result* of the simulation, not asserted.\n\nI should keep the design honest: for ZB variants, state construction rule as \"split-backward list scheduling with the paper's stated structural condition as a check: expected T = m(F+B) if construction achieves zero bubble; if simulation gives T > m(F+B), report the measured bubble from the constructed schedule rather than forcing the formula.\"\n\nPriority rules for ZB need to be precise but design-level. I'll define deterministic priority: (1) prefer dB/F with earliest deadline... Let's define concretely:\n- ZB-1p (ZB-1F1B placement): each device runs the 1F1B microbatch order but, whenever its next 1F1B task is not ready (would be idle), it executes the highest-index available dW task whose operands are resident; ties broken by smaller stage index. Condition m ≥ p−1.\n- ZB-2p (ZB-HV placement): same idle-filling rule, plus the ability to split dW into halves and use the \"virtual backward microbatch\" so that during warmup (stage s idle waiting for its first F chain) the device can execute dW of already-completed F microbatches from *other* placements; no m ≥ p−1 restriction.\n\nHonestly, the design-level description: I'll say \"the schedule is generated by the same greedy idle-filling list scheduler with method-specific ready-set extensions; the exact zero-bubble property is measured, and the expected value m(F+B) is only a hypothesis test target.\"\n\nAlso include an alternative-model fallback: if construction rules can't reach zero bubble for ZB, we report modelled bubble from construction and flag A1 re-anchoring.\n\nAlso handle m=2,4 for ZB-1p: constraint m ≥ p−1 violated for p=8 (needs m≥7) → flag cells; report actual bubble (should be > 0) — and the design must state how monotonicity claim handles those flagged cells (still include in monotonicity check; if failure occurs only in flagged infeasible cells, report as \"constraint-region failure\" not a refutation, per pre-registered rule).\n\nDecision rules:\n\nMonotone improvement (Claim A) per method M ∈ {1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p}:\n- A1: E(m) non-decreasing over full grid, Spearman ρ(E, m) = 1.0 (strictly increasing).\n- A2: consecutive relative step gain ≥ +2% for all consecutive pairs in primary grid (except last pair may be exempt? Let me pre-register: applied to grid excluding saturated pairs; better: require ≥2% gain for every consecutive pair where E(m_k) ≤ 0.90; above 0.90 saturation the requirement relaxes to ≥ +0.5% — hmm, cleaner: require gain ≥ 2% on all pairs, and additionally report the saturation point m* where marginal gain < 5%. Let me set the rule: primary A2 applies on sub-grid m ≤ m_sat where m_sat = largest m with E(m) < 0.90; beyond that, requirement is non-decreasing (A1) only. That avoids failure due to asymptote. Good, pre-register it.\n- A3 (mechanism check): bubble fraction decreases monotonically, and T grows sub-linearly in m (log-log slope < 1), i.e., dT/dm < (F+B).\n\nFailure interpretation: if any method's E decreases or is flat beyond tolerance → the claim is false for that method under the stated model (and would indicate either a non-bubble-dominated cost such as communication, or a schedule-construction bug) → next step: check c/F sensitivity; if failure persists across c/F=0, schedule construction is wrong or claim refuted.\n\nClaim B (ZB-2p strong at low m): for low-m set L = {m : m ≤ p/2} = {2,4} at p=8:\n- B1 primary: min over L of E_ZB-2p ≥ 0.90.\n- B2 secondary: for each m in L: E_ZB-2p/E_1F1B ≥ 1.2 AND E_ZB-2p − E_1F1B ≥ 0.15.\n- B3 context: E_ZB-2p(m=2,4) ≥ E_1F1B(m=16) (small-m ZB-2p beats 4× larger-m 1F1B) — nice corroboration. Let me check plausibility: E_ZB2p ~1.0 vs E_1F1B(16)=0.696 → yes.\n- Failure interpretation: if B1 fails, ZB-2p's construction rule doesn't achieve zero bubble at small m under our stated rule → A1 re-anchoring needed; check memory diagnostic for whether the failure is resource-driven (in-flight activation cap) vs schedule-structure.\n\nMemory diagnostic: from event trace compute per-device max concurrent resident activations A_max(method,m,p) (activation of microbatch i resident on stage s from completion of F_i(s) until completion of B_i(s)), normalized in units of one microbatch's activation memory (bytes = tokens_mb·d_model·layers_per_stage·bytes). Report peak per device; report memory×time too. Note ZB typically higher. Include as caveat column, never dropping cells.\n\nValidation/invariants gating:\nG1: 1F1B simulator reproduces closed-form T = (F+B)(m+p−1) exactly (integer arithmetic) → bubble ratio (p−1)/(m+p−1) for all cells. Only after this passes do we trust ZB schedules.\nG2: 1F1B-I reproduces T = (F+B)(m + (p−1)/v) when v|m and F/v divisible.\nG3 invariants: no device overlap; precedence satisfied; all m·p F-tasks and m·p B-tasks scheduled; per-stage busy time conserved; ZB makespan ≥ m(F+B) lower bound (never below); monotone in m for the lower bound.\nG4: unit test with hand-drawn p=3, m=3 schedule diagram (small enough to verify by hand) for each ZB variant.\nG5: sensitivity stability — decision must hold across the grid; report per-cell table.\n\nSensitivity grid: p ∈ {4,8,16} (primary 8), v ∈ {1,2,4} (primary 2), B/F ∈ {2} plus {1,3}? Researcher approved c/F ∈ {0,0.1,0.3}. I'll include: c/F ∈ {0, 0.1, 0.3}, B/F ∈ {1,2,3}, dW/B split ∈ {0.5} plus {0.33,0.67}, recompute on/off. Total cells count: let me compute: primary full factorial = 4 methods × 6 m × 3 p = 72 cells; sensitivity sweeps add ~. Compute numbers: primary grid 4×6×3? Let's define primary p=8: 4×6 = 24 cells; robustness p∈{4,16}: +48 → 72. Sensitivity: v∈{1,4} for 1F1B-I at p=8 (2×6=12), c/F∈{0.1,0.3} all methods×m×p=8 (4×6×8=192? maybe restrict), B/F∈{1,3} (4×6×3=144?). Keep design-level: report total cells ~ 500, each ~microseconds. Fine — say \"≈ 10^3 cells, each O(m·p·tasks) event simulation; total runtime well under a minute in pure stdlib.\"\n\nFeasibility note: stdlib only (heapq, fractions or integer arithmetic to avoid float rounding — use integer time units: F=2 units? Use integer costs: F=1000, B=2000, c = round(0.1·F)... use integers to make exact equality checks valid), deterministic (no RNG), single-core, seconds-scale, no network/GPU. Also note GPU-transferability: replace simulated T by measured post-warmup iteration median.\n\nWarmup rule explicit: schedule repeated in steady state; drop first iteration (ramp), measure makespan of iteration k=2..; equivalently analytic cyclic schedule steady state (max cycle mean) — for a GPU run: run 1 warmup iteration + 20 measured, use median of measured, exclude iter 1; report min/max as spread.\n\nClaim-to-cell mapping table: required.\n\nAssumptions/uncertainties register: A1..A8 with statuses.\n\nLet me also do a couple of quick feasibility checks with execute_check to support the design (e.g., verify that with integer arithmetic the 1F1B closed form equals event sim? That would be \"running an experiment\" partially... The task is design-only; exploratory sanity checks are allowed. I already did the closed-form probe. Maybe I'll add a tiny probe computing memory-accounting expectations and log-log slope thresholds — optional. Could do a quick check on the memory model counts: 1F1B in-flight max = min(m, p) etc. I could quickly compute 1F1B peak activation count = min(m, p−s) max over s = min(m,p). And GPipe = m. That's standard. Fine, no need to compute.\n\nMaybe run one more quick probe to derive E for candidate ZB lower bound and log-log slope expectations for monotonicity checks, plus a threshold check for B3: E_1F1B(m=16,p=8)=0.696. Already have.\n\nI'll do a single small feasibility check: confirm heapq-based event sim scale (microseconds) for p=16, m=64 to back the \"seconds-scale\" claim. That's a micro-benchmark, not an experiment result. It's cheap and supports feasibility. Let me do a very small simulation stub.\n\nActually keep it simple: measure time for pure-Python loop of ~200k heapq operations, which approximates event sim cost. Let me do that.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "cat > /tmp/feas.py <<'EOF'\nimport heapq, time\n# micro-benchmark: scale of a stdlib event-queue loop comparable to one sim cell\n# tasks per cell ~ m*(p F + p B(+p dW)) ; worst cell p=16, m=64, split-backward => 64*16*3=3072 tasks\ndef bench(n_tasks, p):\n    q=[]; t0=time.perf_counter(); ops=0\n    for it in range(300):  # 300 replicates ~ full sensitivity grid size\n        ready=[(1000+i, i) for i in range(n_tasks)]\n        free=[(0,s) for s in range(p)]\n        heapq.heapify(free); out=0\n        while ready:\n            cost,tid = heapq.heappop(ready)\n            tavail,s = heapq.heappop(free)\n            out = tavail+cost\n            heapq.heappush(free,(out,s)); ops+=1\n    return (time.perf_counter()-t0), ops\nfor n_tasks,p in [(64*16*3,16),(64*8*3,8),(2*4*2,4)]:\n    el,ops=