p−1−s); after warmup, strict alternating 1F-then-1B until cooldown. Schedule generated by list-scheduling under this policy → makespan is an output, and the closed form T = (F+B)(m+p−1) is used only as a *validation oracle*, never inserted.\n- **1F1B-I**: same policy on an expanded virtual pipeline of p·v stages with per-chunk cost F/v, B/v, mapping virtual stage (s, j) → device s; requires v | m for exact chunk alignment (hence the m grid multiples of 4).\n- **ZB-1p** (assumption A1): split-backward construction. Each microbatch's backward on stage s is decomposed into dB_{i,s} (input-gradient, cost B_x, blocks predecessor stage's backward chain) and dW_{i,s} (weight-gradient, cost B_w, B_x + B_w = B, and dW has no successors — it is bubble-fillable). Ready-set = standard 1F1B-ready plus any dW whose operands are resident; the scheduler runs dB/F per the 1F1B order and fills every otherwise-idle slot with the highest-priority available dW (priority: smallest remaining slack, tie → smaller microbatch index). Structural condition for zero bubble: m ≥ p−1 (state as modelled property to be *tested*, not assumed).\n- **ZB-2p** (assumption A1): same split-backward DAG, plus the virtual/stage-split (2-place) extension: dW tasks may be placed on either of two adjacent device placements, and dW of microbatch i may be executed at a stage before all dB of i have completed there (i.e. residency obtained via a virtual/staged backward placement), so idle slots exist that ZB-1p cannot fill at m < p−1. Same idle-filling scheduler; the only difference is the enlarged ready set. Zero-bubble (T = m(F+B)) is the *hypothesis tested*, and if the constructed schedule has T > m(F+B), the measured bubble of the constructed schedule is reported (flag \"A1 re-anchoring needed\").\n\nThis is honest: bubble arises from construction, and both methods are compared on the same DAG + costs with only ready-set extension differing.\n\nNow the metrics:\n- T(method, m, p): post-warmup steady-state makespan of one iteration = simulated completion time of the last task of a steady-state iteration (equivalently, simulate iteration k=2 after one full ramp iteration so the device state entering the iteration matches; for interleaved/split-backward schedules we simulate 2 iterations and measure the inter-iteration period between successive last-completions).\n- E = m·p·(F+B+... )? Careful: perfect time per stage = m(F+B) (each stage does m forwards and m backwards). So E = m(F+B)/T for uniform, non-interleaved. For interleaved with chunking, same total per-stage work = m(F+B), so E definition holds. Good: E = W_stage/T where W_stage = m(F+B) is identical across methods → dimensionless relative efficiency; bubble ratio = 1 − E.\n- Throughput: tokens/s = (m · t_tok)/T_inst where T_inst = T × τ, τ = real seconds per model time unit, derived from stated device throughput MFU assumption: τ = F_time... define F_model_time = 1 unit → 1 unit = F_real seconds; F_real = (per-stage per-microbatch FLOPs)/(device_peak_FLOPs × MFU). Provide formula, and report tokens/s = m·t_tok/(T·F_real). Pass/fail uses only E.\n- Peak activation memory: A_max(method,m,p) = max over devices, over time, of count of microbatch activations resident (born at F completion on that device, freed at B completion on that device; for split backward, freed at dB completion since dW needs only input activations? Actually dW needs input activation and grad output → freed after dW). Use: freed at max(dB, dW) completion = dW completion (dW typically later) → honest: freed when last consumer task (dB or dW) completes. Report as multiples of one microbatch activation; convert to bytes with a stated formula; report as diagnostic column with per-device budget B_mem flag.\n\nDecision rules: as I planned, with saturation-aware step-gain rule.\n\nClaim-to-cell mapping table.\n\nSensitivity grid: \n- primary: p=8, v=2, c/F=0, B/F=2 → 4 methods × 6 m = 24 cells\n- robustness on pipeline depth: p ∈ {4,16} → 48 cells\n- comm: c/F ∈ {0.1,0.3} at p=8 → 48 cells\n- B/F ∈ {1,3} at p=8, c=0 → 48\n- v ∈ {1,4} for 1F1B-I only → 12\n- dW share B_w/B ∈ {0.33,0.67} for ZB only → 24\n- recompute-on variant (B inflated by 1/3 with activation freed early) → 24\nTotal ≈ 228 cells. Each ~ms → <1 s total (benchmark: 300 worst-case cells in 0.56 s).\n\nAlso add replication note: deterministic, single run per cell, no seeds; uncertainty comes from parameter choice handled by sensitivity grid rather than stochastic replication.\n\nFailure interpretation per cell/claim.\n\nAnalysis plan outputs: primary table (method × m at p=8: T, E, bubble, tokens/s, samples/s, A_max, flags), monotonicity table (consecutive gains, ρ), low-m table, sensitivity heat table, per-method throughput-vs-m curve.\n\nStatistical note: no hypothesis testing needed (deterministic model) — thresholds are decision rules, and robustness is assessed by the fraction of sensitivity cells passing (pre-registered: claim holds only if it passes in ≥ 100% of cells excluding c/F=0.3 with p=16? Better: primary claim must pass in the primary grid; robustness graded: \"robust\" if passes in all sensitivity cells, \"conditional\" if passes only where c/F ≤ 0.1 or p ≤ 8, \"fragile\" otherwise).\n\nAssumption register A1–A9 with status and impact.\n\nUncertainties register U1–U7.\n\nTransfer protocol to GPU.\n\nWrite it now, reasonably long but organized. Also mention no results claimed.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# FINAL EXPERIMENT DESIGN (design-only deliverable — no experiment run, no measured numbers claimed)\n\n**Question addressed:** does throughput for 1F1B, 1F1B-I and ZB-1p improve as microbatch count `m` increases, while ZB-2p keeps high throughput at *fewer* microbatches?\n\n**Evidence level (stated up front):** the workspace contains no code (`/workspace` = `task.json` only; no torch/numpy; no GPU; network off). Therefore the experiment is a **deterministic analytical + discrete-event schedule simulation** built from stated construction rules. All pass/fail decisions are model-level properties of the schedules, not hardware measurements. Every model-level number below labelled \"expected\" is threshold calibration from closed forms, **not a result**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What is measured, and how each method is *constructed* (no hard-coded bubble formulas)\n\nCommon model: `p` stages = `p` devices, **one compute stream per device**; `m` microbatches. Per-stage per-microbatch forward cost `F`, backward cost `B = 2F`, activation-hop cost `c` (applied on `F_{i,s}→F_{i,s+1}` and `B_{i,s}→B_{i,s−1}`). Integer time units (`F = 1000`, `B = 2000`, `c = round(ρ·F)`) so makespans are exact and equality-based validation is legitimate.\n\nTask DAG (all methods): `F_{i,s} → F_{i,s+1}`; `F_{i,p−1} → B_{i,p−1}`; `B_{i,s} → B_{i,s−1}`; non-preemptive; single-task-per-device; deterministic tie-break `(ready_time, microbatch_idx, stage_idx)`.\n\n| Method | Construction rule (schedule is an **output**, never assumed) |\n|---|---|\n| **1F1B** | Three-phase policy list-scheduling: stage `s` warmup depth `w_s = min(m, p−1−s)`, then strict alternating 1F→1B, then cooldown. |\n| **1F1B-I** | Same policy on an expanded *virtual* pipeline of `p·v` stages with per-chunk costs `F/v`, `B/v`; virtual stage `(s,j)` maps to device `s`. Requires `v | m` (hence the m-grid). |\n| **ZB-1p** (A1) | Split-backward DAG: `B_{i,s}` decomposed into `dB` (input-grad, cost `B_x`, on the blocking chain) and `dW` (weight-grad, cost `B_w`, `B_x+B_w = B`, **no successors** ⇒ bubble-fillable). Scheduler follows the 1F1B order for F/dB and fills **every otherwise-idle slot** with the highest-priority available `dW` (priority = least slack, tie → smaller microbatch index). Structural zero-bubble condition `m ≥ p−1` is **tested, not asserted**. |\n| **ZB-2p** (A1) | Identical DAG and costs as ZB-1p, plus the virtual / stage-split (\"2-place\") extension: `dW` may be executed on either of two adjacent placements and may start before all `dB` of that microbatch are done at that stage (operands obtained via a virtual/staged backward). Only the **ready set is enlarged**; same idle-filling scheduler. Zero bubble (`T = m(F+B)`) is the **hypothesis tested**. |\n\nBecause only the ready-set extension differs between ZB-1p and ZB-2p, any small-`m` difference is attributable to the placement rule, not to cost assumptions. If a constructed ZB schedule yields `T > m(F+B)`, the **measured bubble of that constructed schedule is reported** and the \"A1 re-anchoring needed\" flag is raised — we never paste in a zero-bubble formula.\n\n## 2. Fixed controls (identical for every method ⇒ fair comparison)\n\nSame total work per iteration (`m` microbatches × same global batch); same per-stage compute (one fixed GPT-style config, `n_layers` divisible by `p`, equal layer split); `B/F = 2`; `c` fixed; recomputation policy fixed (off in primary); non-preemption; single stream per device; same steady-state/warmup rule; same deterministic tie-break; same memory-accounting rule.\n\n## 3. Factors and levels\n\n- **Method** (4): 1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p, ZB-2p.\n- **Microbatch count `m`** (6, approved): **{2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64}** (all multiples of 4 ⇒ `v ∈ {1,2,4}` always divides `m`).\n- **Pipeline depth `p`**: primary **8**; robustness **{4, 16}**.\n- **Interleaving factor `v`**: primary **2**; sensitivity {1, 2, 4}.\n- Sensitivity: `c/F ∈ {0, 0.1, 0.3}`; `B/F ∈ {1, 2, 3}`; `B_w/B ∈ {0.33, 0.5, 0.67}` (ZB only); recompute on/off.\n- Cell count ≈ **228** (primary 24 + depth 48 + comm 48 + B/F 48 + v 12 + dW-share 24 + recompute 24). Deterministic ⇒ one run per cell, no seeds; robustness is assessed over the parameter grid, not stochastic replicates.\n\n## 4. Metrics\n\n1. **Primary: dimensionless relative efficiency** `E = W_stage / T`, with `W_stage = m(F+B)` (identical for all methods) and `T` = steady-state iteration makespan. Bubble ratio `= 1 − E`. Repo defines no convention ⇒ pass/fail uses **only** `E`.\n2. **Reported units (non-decision):** `tokens/s = m·t_tok / (T·τ)`, `samples/s = m·b_mb / (T·τ)`, where `τ` (seconds per model time unit) = `FLOPs_per_stage_microbatch / (peak_device_FLOPs · MFU)` with the device/MFU pair stated as a free constant; and `TFLOP/s` utilisation `= E · MFU_device`.\n3. **Steady-state / warmup rule (pre-registered, transfers to GPU):** simulate **2 iterations**, measure the inter-iteration completion period of iterations `k ≥ 2` (entering device state is then periodic). GPU-transfer equivalent: 1 warmup iteration, then `median` of 20 measured iterations, iteration 1 excluded, with min/max reported as spread. No first-iteration ramp, no optimizer/step time, no I/O or checkpointing.\n4. **Memory diagnostic (never a filter):** from the event trace, `A_max(method,m,p) = max over (device, time)` of resident microbatch activations (born at `F` completion, freed when the last consumer — `dB`/`dW` — completes), reported in multiples of one microbatch activation and in bytes (`t_tok·d_model·layers_per_stage·bytes`), plus a column `exceeds per-device budget?`. Cells are **never dropped**; over-budget cells get a caveat and a re-run at half microbatch size as an appendix.\n\n## 5. Pre-registered decision rules (fixed before any run)\n\nLet `saturation point m*` = largest `m` with `E(m*) < 0.90`.\n\n**Claim A — monotone improvement (1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p), per method:**\n- A-1: `E` strictly increasing in `m` over the full grid; Spearman ρ(E, m) = 1.0.\n- A-2: consecutive step gain `E(m_{k+1})/E(m_k) − 1 ≥ +0.02` for every consecutive pair **with `m_k ≤ m*`**; for pairs beyond `m*` only A-1 (non-decreasing) is required — this prevents an asymptotic-saturation artifact from being read as a refutation.\n- A-3 mechanism check: bubble ratio strictly decreasing, and `T` sub-linear in `m` (`dT/dm < F+B`; log-log slope < 1).\n- Calibration (closed form, **not a result**): at `p=8`, `E_1F1B` = 0.364/0.533/0.696/0.821/0.901 for m = 4/8/16/32/64, step gains +46.7/+30.4/+17.9/+9.9 % ⇒ the +2 % floor sits far above rounding noise and far below the expected effect.\n\n**Claim B — ZB-2p strong at fewer microbatches** (low-m set `L = {m : m ≤ p/2}` = {2,4} at `p=8`):\n- B-1 (primary anchor, approved): `min_{m∈L} E_ZB-2p ≥ 0.90`.\n- B-2 (secondary corroboration): ∀ `m∈L`: `E_ZB-2p / E_1F1B ≥ 1.2` **and** `E_ZB-2p − E_1F1B ≥ 0.15`.\n- B-3 (context statement): `E_ZB-2p(m=2 or 4) ≥ E_1F1B(m=4·m)`, i.e. ZB-2p at low `m` matches or beats 1F1B at 4× its microbatch count (model prediction reference: 1.00 vs 0.696 at m=16).\n- B-4 ordering/no-crossing sanity: `E_ZB-2p ≥ E_ZB-1p ≥ E_1F1B-I > E_1F1B` at every `m`.\n\n**ZB-1p constraint-region rule (approved):** `m = 2, 4` at `p=8` violate `m ≥ p−1` and are kept and **flagged** `INFEASIBLE-REGION`. If Claim A fails for ZB-1p **only** inside flagged cells, the outcome is reported as \"holds in the feasible region, degrades where its structural condition is violated\" — not as a refutation.\n\n**Verdict grades:** `supported` = Claim A (all three methods) ∧ Claim B pass on the primary grid; `robust` = pass in **all** sensitivity cells; `conditional` = pass only under restricted parameters (name the restriction, e.g. `c/F ≤ 0.1`); `not supported` = any primary-grid failure (then state which method/m and the diagnosis from §7).\n\n## 6. Claim-to-cell mapping table\n\n| Half of question | Metric | Cells | Threshold | If it fails, that means |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1F1B improves with more microbatches | `E` (bubble ratio) | 1F1B × m∈{2..64} at p=8 (6 cells) | ρ=1.0 ∧ step gain ≥ +2 % up to `m*` | Bubble term is not the bottleneck (e.g. `c/F` dominating) or construction bug → inspect `c/F` sweep, then §7 gates; genuine flatness ⇒ claim false for 1F1B |\n| 1F1B-I improves with more microbatches | `E` | 1F1B-I (v=2) × 6 m at p=8 | same | Same diagnosis + check `v | m` alignment; failure at v>1 only ⇒ interleaving overhead not modelled ⇒ model gap, flag |\n| ZB-1p improves with more microbatches | `E` | ZB-1p × 6 m at p=8 (m=2,4 flagged) | same, restricted rule for flagged cells | If failing **inside** flagged cells ⇒ structural condition `m≥p−1` binds (expected, reported as such); if failing **outside** ⇒ ready-set construction does not achieve the claimed zero bubble ⇒ A1 re-anchoring |\n| ZB-2p keeps high throughput with **fewer** microbatches (primary) | `E` | ZB-2p × m∈{2,4} at p=8 | `E ≥ 0.90` | The 2-place/virtual extension does not generate fillable idle slots at low `m` ⇒ mechanism assumption A1 wrong; re-anchor before answering |\n| — same, corroborating | `E` ratio & gap | ZB-2p vs 1F1B × m∈{2,4} | ratio ≥ 1.2 ∧ gap ≥ 0.15 | ZB-2p's advantage is not material at low `m`; report the actual ratio, do not claim improvement |\n| — same, contextual | `E` cross-m | ZB-2p m=2,4 vs 1F1B m=8,16 | `E_ZB-2p(low m) ≥ E_1F1B(4×m)` | Advantage smaller than the \"×4 microbatch equivalence\" framing; drop that phrasing from the answer |\n| Whole-question coherence | `E` ordering | all 4 methods × all m | B-4 ordering everywhere | Crossings ⇒ cost model or scheduler asymmetry; investigate before publishing any comparison |\n| Cost of ZB's throughput (caveat, non-decisional) | `A_max` | all cells | none (report-only) | Over-budget cells stay in the table with the memory caveat, plus half-microbatch appendix run |\n\n## 7. Validation gates and invariants (gate before trusting any ZB number)\n\n- **G1 (known-answer gate):** the 1F1B simulator must reproduce the closed form `T = (F+B)(m+p−1)` **exactly** for every primary cell ⇒ bubble ratio `(p−1)/(m+p−1)`. ZB results are not admissible until G1 passes. (Closed-form oracle used only for validation.)\n- **G2:** 1F1B-I reproduces `T = (F+B)(m + (p−1)/v)` for `v | m`.\n- **G3 invariants (all cells):** no device overlap; all `m·p` F and `m·p` B/dB tasks scheduled plus dW where applicable; precedence respected; per-stage busy time conserved (`= m(F+B)` + idle); `T ≥ m(F+B)` always (never below the perfect-pipeline lower bound); `T` non-increasing in `p` at fixed `m` is *not* required (reported, not enforced).\n- **G4 hand-check:** one hand-drawn small case (`p=3, m=3`) per method verified manually against the produced Gantt chart.\n- **G5 sensitivity stability:** the verdict grade from §5 is computed from the fraction of sensitivity cells passing; report the full cell table so a reviewer can see exactly where a claim weakens.\n\n## 8. Analysis outputs\n\n(1) Primary table: method × m at p=8 → `T`, `E`, bubble ratio, tokens/s, samples/s, `A_max`, flags. (2) Monotonicity table: consecutive gains + ρ per method, with `m*`. (3) Low-m table: `E` of all methods at m=2,4 with ratio/gap vs 1F1B (B-1/B-2/B-3 verdicts). (4) Sensitivity heat table across `p`, `v`, `c/F`, `B/F`, `B_w/B`, recompute. (5) Throughput-vs-`m` curve per method (E on primary axis) + bubble-ratio companion. (6) Memory-cost table as the explicit caveat column for ZB claims.\n\n## 9. Feasibility note (verified for scale, no GPU/deps)\n\nPure stdlib (`heapq`, integer arithmetic, `fractions` only if a ratio check is needed); no torch/numpy/GPU/network needed; no RNG. Measured cost proxy for the whole grid on this host: 300 worst-case cells (`p=16, m=64`, split-backward, 3072 tasks/cell) in **0.56 s** (921,600 queue ops) ⇒ the full ~228-cell design runs sub-second; the grid can additionally be sharded over the 224 available cores if desired. Design validity does not depend on hardware: the claim under test is a structural property of schedule geometry (bubble length ∝ `p−1` vs `m`).\n\n**Transfer-to-GPU protocol (unchanged design):** replace simulated `T` by measured post-warmup iteration time (§4.3 rule) and re-run the identical tables, thresholds and claim-to-cell mapping; nothing else changes. Deviations to expect and to report as model scope: kernel variance, stragglers, allocator/fragmentation, memory-forced recompute.\n\n## 10. Assumptions & uncertainties register\n\n**Assumptions (not confirmed by anything in the workspace):** A1 ZB-1p = split-backward/1-place variant with structural zero-bubble condition; ZB-2p = virtual/stage-split 2-place variant targeting near-zero bubble at any `m` — **primary interpretation as instructed; if repo semantics differ, Claim B's decision rule must be re-anchored** (and ZB numbers re-derived from the real ready set). A2 m-grid and `v` are designer choices, not repo defaults. A3 `E` is the decision metric because the repo defines none. A4 `B = 2F`, equal layer split, single stream per device, fixed `c`. A5 memory is diagnostic-only, never a filter. A6 steady-state makespan with 1-iteration warmup exclusion. A7 activation freed at last-consumer completion. A8 the repo ships *some* implementation of these names but its files/symbols/args are unverifiable here. A9 device throughput/MFU used only for unit reporting.\n\n**Uncertainties:** U1 real repo naming/args and any built-in cost model (unknown). U2 whether ZB-1p's true condition is `m ≥ p−1` (modelled, tested). U3 whether ZB-2p's zero bubble holds at `m < p−1` in the real implementation (the crux of the question — resolved only by the construction test / source access). U4 comm time inclusion in the repo's schedules (covered, not resolved, by `c/F`). U5 memory-forced recompute could erase part of ZB's low-`m` advantage in a real run. U6 absolute tokens/s scale is arbitrary here and carries no claim. U7 model pass/fail may not transfer 1:1 to hardware (see §9).\n\n**Deliverable status:** design complete; no experiment executed; no measured or simulated outcome is claimed anywhere in this document.", "evidence": "**Repository/environment inspection (actually run):**\n1. `inspect_repository(\".\")` and `ls -la /workspace` → single file `task.json` (651 B); no source tree, git, tests, examples, requirements.\n2. `cat /workspace/task.json` → question + context + \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"\n3. `find / -xdev -iname \"*zero*bubble*\" -o -iname \"*pipe*parallel*\"` → 0 matches; `grep -ril \"1f1b\" /usr/lib/python3*/ /usr/local/lib` → only hex false positives (idna uts46data.py, ansible FILES.json/poetry.lock) ⇒ no 1F1B/ZB code reachable; hence file+symbol entry points, microbatch-count argument name, built-in simulator presence, throughput convention, default p/device count, reusable harness = all reported UNKNOWN.\n4. `python3 --version` = 3.14.4; `pip3 list` = Ubuntu system packages only; `import torch`/`import numpy` → ModuleNotFoundError; gpu_enabled=false, network disabled ⇒ stdlib-only harness mandated.\n\n**Threshold calibration (closed-form arithmetic probe, /tmp/sanity.py, exit 0 — explicitly not a result):** relative efficiency m/(m+p−1) at p=8: 0.364 (m=4), 0.533 (8), 0.696 (16), 0.821 (32), 0.901 (64); consecutive gains +46.7 %, +30.4 %, +17.9 %, +9.9 %; interleaved m/(m+(p−1)/v): v=2 → 0.533→0.948, v=4 → 0.696→0.973; p=4 1F1B → 0.571→0.955. This places the +2 % step-gain floor above numerical noise and ~5–20× below the expected effect, and shows m=4 at p=8 is a discriminating low-m cell (E_1F1B = 0.364 vs ZB target ≥ 0.90).\n\n**Feasibility/scale check (stdlib micro-benchmark, /tmp/feas.py, exit 0):** 300 replicates of a worst-case event-queue cell (3072 tasks, p=16) → 0.56 s / 921,600 heapq ops; p=8 variant → 0.26 s. Extrapolating to the ~228-cell grid ⇒ sub-second single-core runtime with only heapq/integer arithmetic, i.e. the design is executable in this exact environment (224 cores, no GPU, no network, no third-party packages).\n\n**Design-integrity choices tied to repo observations:** because the repo defines no metric convention, pass/fail is anchored on the dimensionless E (identical per-stage work W_stage = m(F+B) across methods makes the comparison fair by construction); because no harness exists to reuse, validation is built in as gating known-answer checks (1F1B must exactly reproduce (F+B)(m+p−1) before any ZB number is admissible) plus structural invariants (no device overlap, precedence, T ≥ m(F+B)); ZB bubble is produced by stated construction rules (split-backward DAG + idle-filling list scheduler, ZB-2p differing only by an enlarged ready set) rather than pasted formulas, so a failed construction is reported as a modelled failure instead of being hidden.", "assumptions": ["A1: ZB-1p = split-backward single-placement variant needing a structural condition (modelled m>=p-1) for zero bubble, ZB-2p = virtual/stage-split two-placement variant targeting near-zero bubble for any m; both are the instructed primary interpretation but remain UNCONFIRMED — if repo semantics differ, Claim B's thresholds must be re-anchored to the real ready set", "A2: m grid {2,4,8,16,32,64}, v=2 primary, p=8 primary are designer choices (repo defaults unknown)", "A3: decision metric = dimensionless relative efficiency E = m(F+B)/T because no repo metric exists; tokens/s and samples/s are reporting-only", "A4: cost model B=2F, integer unit times, fixed per-hop comm cost c, single compute stream per device, equal layer split, recomputation off in the primary grid", "A5: peak activation memory is a reported diagnostic (freed at last-consumer completion), never an eligibility filter; no cell is dropped", "A6: post-warmup steady-state makespan (simulate 2 iterations, measure k>=2), equivalent to 1 warmup + median of 20 measured iterations on GPU; optimizer step, I/O and checkpointing excluded", "A7: total per-stage work m(F+B) is identical for all methods, so throughput differences reflect schedule geometry only", "A8: the repository presumably implements these four schedules, but none of its files, symbols, arguments or defaults could be verified in this workspace", "A9: device peak FLOPs and MFU used only to convert E into tokens/s are free constants carrying no claim"], "uncertainties": ["Repo entry points, microbatch-count argument name, default pipeline depth/device count, native throughput convention and any built-in simulator remain UNKNOWN (no code present)", "Whether ZB-1p's real zero-bubble condition is m>=p-1 and whether ZB-2p truly reaches zero bubble for m<p-1 — this is the crux of the question and is resolved only by the construction test or by restored source access", "Whether the repo's schedules charge activation-communication time; the c/F in {0,0.1,0.3} sweep bounds but does not settle this", "Memory pressure / memory-forced recompute could reduce ZB's low-m advantage in a real GPU run (model under-states allocator and fragmentation effects)", "Interleaved-schedule divisibility and per-chunk overheads are idealised (F/v, B/v); real interleaved implementations add cross-chunk handoff cost", "Model-level pass/fail may not transfer one-to-one to hardware due to kernel variance, stragglers and non-pipeline overheads; the GPU-transfer protocol keeps metrics and thresholds identical but cannot guarantee identical verdicts", "Threshold choices (+2% step gain, E>=0.90, 1.2x and 0.15 gap) are pre-registered conventions; a different reviewer-sanctioned threshold could change a borderline 'conditional' verdict"], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a78bd3fdcfacb05e", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-a78bd3fdcfacb05e", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"answer\": \"# FINAL EXPERIMENT DESIGN (design-only deliverable \\u2014 no experiment run, no measured numbers claimed)\\n\\n**Question addressed:** does throughput for 1F1B, 1F1B-I and ZB-1p improve as microbatch count `m` increases, while ZB-2p keeps high throughput at *fewer* microbatches?\\n\\n**Evidence level (stated up front):** the workspace contains no code (`/workspace` = `task.json` only; no torch/numpy; no GPU; network off). Therefore the experiment is a **deterministic analytical + discrete-event schedule simulation** built from stated construction rules. All pass/fail decisions are model-level properties of the schedules, not hardware measurements. Every model-level number below labelled \\\"expected\\\" is threshold calibration from closed forms, **not a result**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. What is measured, and how each method is *constructed* (no hard-coded bubble formulas)\\n\\nCommon model: `p` stages = `p` devices, **one compute stream per device**; `m` microbatches. Per-stage per-microbatch forward cost `F`, backward cost `B = 2F`, activation-hop cost `c` (applied on `F_{i,s}\\u2192F_{i,s+1}` and `B_{i,s}\\u2192B_{i,s\\u22121}`). Integer time units (`F = 1000`, `B = 2000`, `c = round(\\u03c1\\u00b7F)`) so makespans are exact and equality-based validation is legitimate.\\n\\nTask DAG (all methods): `F_{i,s} \\u2192 F_{i,s+1}`; `F_{i,p\\u22121} \\u2192 B_{i,p\\u22121}`; `B_{i,s} \\u2192 B_{i,s\\u22121}`; non-preemptive; single-task-per-device; deterministic tie-break `(ready_time, microbatch_idx, stage_idx)`.\\n\\n| Method | Construction rule (schedule is an **output**, never assumed) |\\n|---|---|\\n| **1F1B** | Three-phase policy list-scheduling: stage `s` warmup depth `w_s = min(m, p\\u22121\\u2212s)`, then strict alternating 1F\\u21921B, then cooldown. |\\n| **1F1B-I** | Same policy on an expanded *virtual* pipeline of `p\\u00b7v` stages with per-chunk costs `F/v`, `B/v`; virtual stage `(s,j)` maps to device `s`. Requires `v | m` (hence the m-grid). |\\n| **ZB-1p** (A1) | Split-backward DAG: `B_{i,s}` decomposed into `dB` (input-grad, cost `B_x`, on the blocking chain) and `dW` (weight-grad, cost `B_w`, `B_x+B_w = B`, **no successors** \\u21d2 bubble-fillable). Scheduler follows the 1F1B order for F/dB and fills **every otherwise-idle slot** with the highest-priority available `dW` (priority = least slack, tie \\u2192 smaller microbatch index). Structural zero-bubble condition `m \\u2265 p\\u22121` is **tested, not asserted**. |\\n| **ZB-2p** (A1) | Identical DAG and costs as ZB-1p, plus the virtual / stage-split (\\\"2-place\\\") extension: `dW` may be executed on either of two adjacent placements and may start before all `dB` of that microbatch are done at that stage (operands obtained via a virtual/staged backward). Only the **ready set is enlarged**; same idle-filling scheduler. Zero bubble (`T = m(F+B)`) is the **hypothesis tested**. |\\n\\nBecause only the ready-set extension differs between ZB-1p and ZB-2p, any small-`m` difference is attributable to the placement rule, not to cost assumptions. If a constructed ZB schedule yields `T > m(F+B)`, the **measured bubble of that constructed schedule is reported** and the \\\"A1 re-anchoring needed\\\" flag is raised \\u2014 we never paste in a zero-bubble formula.\\n\\n## 2. Fixed controls (identical for every method \\u21d2 fair comparison)\\n\\nSame total work per iteration (`m` microbatches \\u00d7 same global batch); same per-stage compute (one fixed GPT-style config, `n_layers` divisible by `p`, equal layer split); `B/F = 2`; `c` fixed; recomputation policy fixed (off in primary); non-preemption; single stream per device; same steady-state/warmup rule; same deterministic tie-break; same memory-accounting rule.\\n\\n## 3. Factors and levels\\n\\n- **Method** (4): 1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p, ZB-2p.\\n- **Microbatch count `m`** (6, approved): **{2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64}** (all multiples of 4 \\u21d2 `v \\u2208 {1,2,4}` always divides `m`).\\n- **Pipeline depth `p`**: primary **8**; robustness **{4, 16}**.\\n- **Interleaving factor `v`**: primary **2**; sensitivity {1, 2, 4}.\\n- Sensitivity: `c/F \\u2208 {0, 0.1, 0.3}`; `B/F \\u2208 {1, 2, 3}`; `B_w/B \\u2208 {0.33, 0.5, 0.67}` (ZB only); recompute on/off.\\n- Cell count \\u2248 **228** (primary 24 + depth 48 + comm 48 + B/F 48 + v 12 + dW-share 24 + recompute 24). Deterministic \\u21d2 one run per cell, no seeds; robustness is assessed over the parameter grid, not stochastic replicates.\\n\\n## 4. Metrics\\n\\n1. **Primary: dimensionless relative efficiency** `E = W_stage / T`, with `W_stage = m(F+B)` (identical for all methods) and `T` = steady-state iteration makespan. Bubble ratio `= 1 \\u2212 E`. Repo defines no convention \\u21d2 pass/fail uses **only** `E`.\\n2. **Reported units (non-decision):** `tokens/s = m\\u00b7t_tok / (T\\u00b7\\u03c4)`, `samples/s = m\\u00b7b_mb / (T\\u00b7\\u03c4)`, where `\\u03c4` (seconds per model time unit) = `FLOPs_per_stage_microbatch / (peak_device_FLOPs \\u00b7 MFU)` with the device/MFU pair stated as a free constant; and `TFLOP/s` utilisation `= E \\u00b7 MFU_device`.\\n3. **Steady-state / warmup rule (pre-registered, transfers to GPU):** simulate **2 iterations**, measure the inter-iteration completion period of iterations `k \\u2265 2` (entering device state is then periodic). GPU-transfer equivalent: 1 warmup iteration, then `median` of 20 measured iterations, iteration 1 excluded, with min/max reported as spread. No first-iteration ramp, no optimizer/step time, no I/O or checkpointing.\\n4. **Memory diagnostic (never a filter):** from the event trace, `A_max(method,m,p) = max over (device, time)` of resident microbatch activations (born at `F` completion, freed when the last consumer \\u2014 `dB`/`dW` \\u2014 completes), reported in multiples of one microbatch activation and in bytes (`t_tok\\u00b7d_model\\u00b7layers_per_stage\\u00b7bytes`), plus a column `exceeds per-device budget?`. Cells are **never dropped**; over-budget cells get a caveat and a re-run at half microbatch size as an appendix.\\n\\n## 5. Pre-registered decision rules (fixed before any run)\\n\\nLet `saturation point m*` = largest `m` with `E(m*) < 0.90`.\\n\\n**Claim A \\u2014 monotone improvement (1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p), per method:**\\n- A-1: `E` strictly increasing in `m` over the full grid; Spearman \\u03c1(E, m) = 1.0.\\n- A-2: consecutive step gain `E(m_{k+1})/E(m_k) \\u2212 1 \\u2265 +0.02` for every consecutive pair **with `m_k \\u2264 m*`**; for pairs beyond `m*` only A-1 (non-decreasing) is required \\u2014 this prevents an asymptotic-saturation artifact from being read as a refutation.\\n- A-3 mechanism check: bubble ratio strictly decreasing, and `T` sub-linear in `m` (`dT/dm < F+B`; log-log slope < 1).\\n- Calibration (closed form, **not a result**): at `p=8`, `E_1F1B` = 0.364/0.533/0.696/0.821/0.901 for m = 4/8/16/32/64, step gains +46.7/+30.4/+17.9/+9.9 % \\u21d2 the +2 % floor sits far above rounding noise and far below the expected effect.\\n\\n**Claim B \\u2014 ZB-2p strong at fewer microbatches** (low-m set `L = {m : m \\u2264 p/2}` = {2,4} at `p=8`):\\n- B-1 (primary anchor, approved): `min_{m\\u2208L} E_ZB-2p \\u2265 0.90`.\\n- B-2 (secondary corroboration): \\u2200 `m\\u2208L`: `E_ZB-2p / E_1F1B \\u2265 1.2` **and** `E_ZB-2p \\u2212 E_1F1B \\u2265 0.15`.\\n- B-3 (context statement): `E_ZB-2p(m=2 or 4) \\u2265 E_1F1B(m=4\\u00b7m)`, i.e. ZB-2p at low `m` matches or beats 1F1B at 4\\u00d7 its microbatch count (model prediction reference: 1.00 vs 0.696 at m=16).\\n- B-4 ordering/no-crossing sanity: `E_ZB-2p \\u2265 E_ZB-1p \\u2265 E_1F1B-I > E_1F1B` at every `m`.\\n\\n**ZB-1p constraint-region rule (approved):** `m = 2, 4` at `p=8` violate `m \\u2265 p\\u22121` and are kept and **flagged** `INFEASIBLE-REGION`. If Claim A fails for ZB-1p **only** inside flagged cells, the outcome is reported as \\\"holds in the feasible region, degrades where its structural condition is violated\\\" \\u2014 not as a refutation.\\n\\n**Verdict grades:** `supported` = Claim A (all three methods) \\u2227 Claim B pass on the primary grid; `robust` = pass in **all** sensitivity cells; `conditional` = pass only under restricted parameters (name the restriction, e.g. `c/F \\u2264 0.1`); `not supported` = any primary-grid failure (then state which method/m and the diagnosis from \\u00a77).\\n\\n## 6. Claim-to-cell mapping table\\n\\n| Half of question | Metric | Cells | Threshold | If it fails, that means |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1F1B improves with more microbatches | `E` (bubble ratio) | 1F1B \\u00d7 m\\u2208{2..64} at p=8 (6 cells) | \\u03c1=1.0 \\u2227 step gain \\u2265 +2 % up to `m*` | Bubble term is not the bottleneck (e.g. `c/F` dominating) or construction bug \\u2192 inspect `c/F` sweep, then \\u00a77 gates; genuine flatness \\u21d2 claim false for 1F1B |\\n| 1F1B-I improves with more microbatches | `E` | 1F1B-I (v=2) \\u00d7 6 m at p=8 | same | Same diagnosis + check `v | m` alignment; failure at v>1 only \\u21d2 interleaving overhead not modelled \\u21d2 model gap, flag |\\n| ZB-1p improves with more microbatches | `E` | ZB-1p \\u00d7 6 m at p=8 (m=2,4 flagged) | same, restricted rule for flagged cells | If failing **inside** flagged cells \\u21d2 structural condition `m\\u2265p\\u22121` binds (expected, reported as such); if failing **outside** \\u21d2 ready-set construction does not achieve the claimed zero bubble \\u21d2 A1 re-anchoring |\\n| ZB-2p keeps high throughput with **fewer** microbatches (primary) | `E` | ZB-2p \\u00d7 m\\u2208{2,4} at p=8 | `E \\u2265 0.90` | The 2-place/virtual extension does not generate fillable idle slots at low `m` \\u21d2 mechanism assumption A1 wrong; re-anchor before answering |\\n| \\u2014 same, corroborating | `E` ratio & gap | ZB-2p vs 1F1B \\u00d7 m\\u2208{2,4} | ratio \\u2265 1.2 \\u2227 gap \\u2265 0.15 | ZB-2p's advantage is not material at low `m`; report the actual ratio, do not claim improvement |\\n| \\u2014 same, contextual | `E` cross-m | ZB-2p m=2,4 vs 1F1B m=8,16 | `E_ZB-2p(low m) \\u2265 E_1F1B(4\\u00d7m)` | Advantage smaller than the \\\"\\u00d74 microbatch equivalence\\\" framing; drop that phrasing from the answer |\\n| Whole-question coherence | `E` ordering | all 4 methods \\u00d7 all m | B-4 ordering everywhere | Crossings \\u21d2 cost model or scheduler asymmetry; investigate before publishing any comparison |\\n| Cost of ZB's throughput (caveat, non-decisional) | `A_max` | all cells | none (report-only) | Over-budget cells stay in the table with the memory caveat, plus half-microbatch appendix run |\\n\\n## 7. Validation gates and invariants (gate before trusting any ZB number)\\n\\n- **G1 (known-answer gate):** the 1F1B simulator must reproduce the closed form `T = (F+B)(m+p\\u22121)` **exactly** for every primary cell \\u21d2 bubble ratio `(p\\u22121)/(m+p\\u22121)`. ZB results are not admissible until G1 passes. (Closed-form oracle used only for validation.)\\n- **G2:** 1F1B-I reproduces `T = (F+B)(m + (p\\u22121)/v)` for `v | m`.\\n- **G3 invariants (all cells):** no device overlap; all `m\\u00b7p` F and `m\\u00b7p` B/dB tasks scheduled plus dW where applicable; precedence respected; per-stage busy time conserved (`= m(F+B)` + idle); `T \\u2265 m(F+B)` always (never below the perfect-pipeline lower bound); `T` non-increasing in `p` at fixed `m` is *not* required (reported, not enforced).\\n- **G4 hand-check:** one hand-drawn small case (`p=3, m=3`) per method verified manually against the produced Gantt chart.\\n- **G5 sensitivity stability:** the verdict grade from \\u00a75 is computed from the fraction of sensitivity cells passing; report the full cell table so a reviewer can see exactly where a claim weakens.\\n\\n## 8. Analysis outputs\\n\\n(1) Primary table: method \\u00d7 m at p=8 \\u2192 `T`, `E`, bubble ratio, tokens/s, samples/s, `A_max`, flags. (2) Monotonicity table: consecutive gains + \\u03c1 per method, with `m*`. (3) Low-m table: `E` of all methods at m=2,4 with ratio/gap vs 1F1B (B-1/B-2/B-3 verdicts). (4) Sensitivity heat table across `p`, `v`, `c/F`, `B/F`, `B_w/B`, recompute. (5) Throughput-vs-`m` curve per method (E on primary axis) + bubble-ratio companion. (6) Memory-cost table as the explicit caveat column for ZB claims.\\n\\n## 9. Feasibility note (verified for scale, no GPU/deps)\\n\\nPure stdlib (`heapq`, integer arithmetic, `fractions` only if a ratio check is needed); no torch/numpy/GPU/network needed; no RNG. Measured cost proxy for the whole grid on this host: 300 worst-case cells (`p=16, m=64`, split-backward, 3072 tasks/cell) in **0.56 s** (921,600 queue ops) \\u21d2 the full ~228-cell design runs sub-second; the grid can additionally be sharded over the 224 available cores if desired. Design validity does not depend on hardware: the claim under test is a structural property of schedule geometry (bubble length \\u221d `p\\u22121` vs `m`).\\n\\n**Transfer-to-GPU protocol (unchanged design):** replace simulated `T` by measured post-warmup iteration time (\\u00a74.3 rule) and re-run the identical tables, thresholds and claim-to-cell mapping; nothing else changes. Deviations to expect and to report as model scope: kernel variance, stragglers, allocator/fragmentation, memory-forced recompute.\\n\\n## 10. Assumptions & uncertainties register\\n\\n**Assumptions (not confirmed by anything in the workspace):** A1 ZB-1p = split-backward/1-place variant with structural zero-bubble condition; ZB-2p = virtual/stage-split 2-place variant targeting near-zero bubble at any `m` \\u2014 **primary interpretation as instructed; if repo semantics differ, Claim B's decision rule must be re-anchored** (and ZB numbers re-derived from the real ready set). A2 m-grid and `v` are designer choices, not repo defaults. A3 `E` is the decision metric because the repo defines none. A4 `B = 2F`, equal layer split, single stream per device, fixed `c`. A5 memory is diagnostic-only, never a filter. A6 steady-state makespan with 1-iteration warmup exclusion. A7 activation freed at last-consumer completion. A8 the repo ships *some* implementation of these names but its files/symbols/args are unverifiable here. A9 device throughput/MFU used only for unit reporting.\\n\\n**Uncertainties:** U1 real repo naming/args and any built-in cost model (unknown). U2 whether ZB-1p's true condition is `m \\u2265 p\\u22121` (modelled, tested). U3 whether ZB-2p's zero bubble holds at `m < p\\u22121` in the real implementation (the crux of the question \\u2014 resolved only by the construction test / source access). U4 comm time inclusion in the repo's schedules (covered, not resolved, by `c/F`). U5 memory-forced recompute could erase part of ZB's low-`m` advantage in a real run. U6 absolute tokens/s scale is arbitrary here and carries no claim. U7 model pass/fail may not transfer 1:1 to hardware (see \\u00a79).\\n\\n**Deliverable status:** design complete; no experiment executed; no measured or simulated outcome is claimed anywhere in this document.\", \"evidence\": \"**Repository/environment inspection (actually run):**\\n1. `inspect_repository(\\\".\\\")` and `ls -la /workspace` \\u2192 single file `task.json` (651 B); no source tree, git, tests, examples, requirements.\\n2. `cat /workspace/task.json` \\u2192 question + context + \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"\\n3. `find / -xdev -iname \\\"*zero*bubble*\\\" -o -iname \\\"*pipe*parallel*\\\"` \\u2192 0 matches; `grep -ril \\\"1f1b\\\" /usr/lib/python3*/ /usr/local/lib` \\u2192 only hex false positives (idna uts46data.py, ansible FILES.json/poetry.lock) \\u21d2 no 1F1B/ZB code reachable; hence file+symbol entry points, microbatch-count argument name, built-in simulator presence, throughput convention, default p/device count, reusable harness = all reported UNKNOWN.\\n4. `python3 --version` = 3.14.4; `pip3 list` = Ubuntu system packages only; `import torch`/`import numpy` \\u2192 ModuleNotFoundError; gpu_enabled=false, network disabled \\u21d2 stdlib-only harness mandated.\\n\\n**Threshold calibration (closed-form arithmetic probe, /tmp/sanity.py, exit 0 \\u2014 explicitly not a result):** relative efficiency m/(m+p\\u22121) at p=8: 0.364 (m=4), 0.533 (8), 0.696 (16), 0.821 (32), 0.901 (64); consecutive gains +46.7 %, +30.4 %, +17.9 %, +9.9 %; interleaved m/(m+(p\\u22121)/v): v=2 \\u2192 0.533\\u21920.948, v=4 \\u2192 0.696\\u21920.973; p=4 1F1B \\u2192 0.571\\u21920.955. This places the +2 % step-gain floor above numerical noise and ~5\\u201320\\u00d7 below the expected effect, and shows m=4 at p=8 is a discriminating low-m cell (E_1F1B = 0.364 vs ZB target \\u2265 0.90).\\n\\n**Feasibility/scale check (stdlib micro-benchmark, /tmp/feas.py, exit 0):** 300 replicates of a worst-case event-queue cell (3072 tasks, p=16) \\u2192 0.56 s / 921,600 heapq ops; p=8 variant \\u2192 0.26 s. Extrapolating to the ~228-cell grid \\u21d2 sub-second single-core runtime with only heapq/integer arithmetic, i.e. the design is executable in this exact environment (224 cores, no GPU, no network, no third-party packages).\\n\\n**Design-integrity choices tied to repo observations:** because the repo defines no metric convention, pass/fail is anchored on the dimensionless E (identical per-stage work W_stage = m(F+B) across methods makes the comparison fair by construction); because no harness exists to reuse, validation is built in as gating known-answer checks (1F1B must exactly reproduce (F+B)(m+p\\u22121) before any ZB number is admissible) plus structural invariants (no device overlap, precedence, T \\u2265 m(F+B)); ZB bubble is produced by stated construction rules (split-backward DAG + idle-filling list scheduler, ZB-2p differing only by an enlarged ready set) rather than pasted formulas, so a failed construction is reported as a modelled failure instead of being hidden.\", \"assumptions\": [\"A1: ZB-1p = split-backward single-placement variant needing a structural condition (modelled m>=p-1) for zero bubble, ZB-2p = virtual/stage-split two-placement variant targeting near-zero bubble for any m; both are the instructed primary interpretation but remain UNCONFIRMED \\u2014 if repo semantics differ, Claim B's thresholds must be re-anchored to the real ready set\", \"A2: m grid {2,4,8,16,32,64}, v=2 primary, p=8 primary are designer choices (repo defaults unknown)\", \"A3: decision metric = dimensionless relative efficiency E = m(F+B)/T because no repo metric exists; tokens/s and samples/s are reporting-only\", \"A4: cost model B=2F, integer unit times, fixed per-hop comm cost c, single compute stream per device, equal layer split, recomputation off in the primary grid\", \"A5: peak activation memory is a reported diagnostic (freed at last-consumer completion), never an eligibility filter; no cell is dropped\", \"A6: post-warmup steady-state makespan (simulate 2 iterations, measure k>=2), equivalent to 1 warmup + median of 20 measured iterations on GPU; optimizer step, I/O and checkpointing excluded\", \"A7: total per-stage work m(F+B) is identical for all methods, so throughput differences reflect schedule geometry only\", \"A8: the repository presumably implements these four schedules, but none of its files, symbols, arguments or defaults could be verified in this workspace\", \"A9: device peak FLOPs and MFU used only to convert E into tokens/s are free constants carrying no claim\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Repo entry points, microbatch-count argument name, default pipeline depth/device count, native throughput convention and any built-in simulator remain UNKNOWN (no code present)\", \"Whether ZB-1p's real zero-bubble condition is m>=p-1 and whether ZB-2p truly reaches zero bubble for m<p-1 \\u2014 this is the crux of the question and is resolved only by the construction test or by restored source access\", \"Whether the repo's schedules charge activation-communication time; the c/F in {0,0.1,0.3} sweep bounds but does not settle this\", \"Memory pressure / memory-forced recompute could reduce ZB's low-m advantage in a real GPU run (model under-states allocator and fragmentation effects)\", \"Interleaved-schedule divisibility and per-chunk overheads are idealised (F/v, B/v); real interleaved implementations add cross-chunk handoff cost\", \"Model-level pass/fail may not transfer one-to-one to hardware due to kernel variance, stragglers and non-pipeline overheads; the GPU-transfer protocol keeps metrics and thresholds identical but cannot guarantee identical verdicts\", \"Threshold choices (+2% step gain, E>=0.90, 1.2x and 0.15 gap) are pre-registered conventions; a different reviewer-sanctioned threshold could change a borderline 'conditional' verdict\"], \"summary\": \"Full design: schedule-simulation experiment, controls, thresholds, claim mapping\", \"stage\": \"result\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-4f3024c1-561c-47af-b09f-d68348138308", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Full design: schedule-simulation experiment, controls, thresholds, claim mapping", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
